JIT: a return whose call-return stack prediction holds falls through to its ret

A guest return pops the pair its call pushed on the call-return stack (guest return address, host return PC) and
compares the address with the one it is returning to. When they match it ends in "ret <host PC>", which the host's
return predictor follows. The L1 lookup for the other case, eight instructions, sat between the compare and the ret,
so every return branched over it:

    ldp x10, x11, [x17], #16 ; sub x10, x10, x6 ; cbz x10, 1f ; <L1 lookup> ; 1: ret x11

The lookup is now emitted after the unit's code (PendingReturnLookups, like the link thunks), and the return is

    ldp x10, x11, [x17], #16 ; sub x10, x10, x6 ; cbnz x10, <lookup> ; ret x11

The lookup is the same instructions in the same order and ends in the same ret, so a missed prediction costs one
taken branch more and nothing else changes. With Highball's call-return epoch (HB_FEX_CALLRET_EPOCH=1) both of its
compares branch to the lookup. SQUARK_FEX_RET_LOOKUP_INLINE=1 puts the lookup back between the compare and the ret.

Why: code with a large footprint is limited by instruction delivery under FEX, not by the instructions themselves.
x64shape's foot_direct (squark-private tools/msfs-harness: N small functions called directly in a fixed shuffled
order) takes 2.2 ns a call with 16 functions and 12.9 ns with 65,536, where Instruments' CPU Counters put 94 % of
the pipeline's slots under "instruction delivery", three quarters of that latency from the instruction cache
(squark records 25.82). Every return carried 32 bytes it never ran and one more taken branch than native code.

foot_direct, ns per call, the fastest of three alternated rounds, this patch alone against the same DLL with the
switch (functions: before -> after): 1,024: 2.36 -> 2.32; 4,096: 3.19 -> 2.58; 16,384: 4.65 -> 4.74;
65,536: 13.31 -> 12.14. Checksums unchanged.

